# Memory Protocol

This rule tells agents how to use the Memory MCP server for persistent knowledge across sessions.

> **Reach for memory deliberately.** Several memory actions are folded into
> action-dispatch tools: `record`/`recall`/`search` stay top-level, but skills,
> relationships, and session handoff/pickup now live under `skill(action=...)`,
> `relate(action=...)`, and `session(action=...)`. Because these are no longer
> standalone tool *names* in your tool list, it is easy to forget they exist —
> treat "save a skill", "link entries", "hand off", and especially **"offer to
> note this"** as first-class reflexes, not afterthoughts. When you discover a
> gotcha/limitation or the user shares a finding, proactively offer to record it
> (see "Notes and Annotations" below) rather than waiting to be asked.

## Result affordances (how the tools guide you)

Memory's tools are built to steer a session, not just return data. Lean on these:

- **`next` hints.** Every result carries an advisory `next` string naming the
  natural follow-on call (e.g. after `record`, "link related work…"; after
  `search`, "open a hit with `recall(entry_id=…)`"). Follow it — it is the
  fastest path through the tree, the same convention the `codebase` action API uses.
- **`dry_run` rehearsal.** `record`, `move`, `discard`, and `relate(merge|bulk)`
  accept `dry_run=true` to preview the effect (where a `record` would land, what a
  `merge` would reparent/redirect) without writing. Reach for `record(dry_run=true)`
  when you are unsure which parent a new entry belongs under — it echoes the
  resolved parent and `suggested_parents`, so you record it correctly the first time.
- **Actionable errors.** A rejected call carries the fix, not just the complaint:
  a dangling `parent_id` returns `suggested_parents`, a mistyped `type` returns the
  valid set plus a `did_you_mean`, and a not-found id points you at `search`. Read
  the `next`/suggestion and retry rather than guessing.
- **`inbox`.** Call `inbox()` to triage the tree read-only — open phases missing a
  checkpoint, entries whose pinned code drifted, and near-duplicate merge candidates,
  each with its own `next`. A good periodic "what needs attention?" check.
  `session(action='pickup')` already folds a one-line `inbox` digest (family counts +
  the top item) into its payload, so the session-start signal reaches you without a
  separate call.

## When to Write

Record to memory when:

- The user says "remember this", "save this", "record", "log this decision"
- A significant design decision is made (even implicitly — use type "annotation")
- An experiment succeeds or fails
- **A plan is confirmed and you begin implementation** — record it as type='plan' with the plan title and a brief scope summary, placed under the active phase
- A checkpoint is reached
- You learn a reusable procedure or pattern (save as a skill)

## Committing Code Writes to Memory

**MANDATORY: Every git commit MUST be preceded by a memory record.** Recording what happened is a
built-in step of committing, not an optional extra — never run `git commit` without first calling
`record()`.

When the user asks you to commit and/or push code (e.g. "commit and push", "commit this", "push it up"):

1. **First, record what happened — _before_ you run git.** Write one concise entry capturing what
   this commit represents. Choose the type that fits:
   - `decision` — a design/approach choice made during the work
   - `checkpoint` — a state summary (what's done, what's next) for completed work
   - `experiment` — something tried with a pass/fail outcome
   - `note` — a smaller observation or change summary

   Place it under the most specific related entry (active phase or deeper), and pass the changed
   files in `files` so it is git-pinned to the code being committed. Do this automatically — never
   stop to ask the user whether to record.
2. **Then run git** (add/commit/push). The entry you just wrote is committed automatically (as
   `memory-mcp`) and rides up with your push, so the code commit and its memory record travel
   together.

**The ONLY exception** is a commit touching zero substantive code or docs — a pure `.gitignore`
tweak, a formatting-only linter fix, or a WIP/"commit-for-safety" snapshot. If you are unsure
whether a commit is substantive, it is — record it.

Never write a second entry for work you already recorded earlier this session (avoid duplicates).
But if the commit includes additional unrecorded work beyond what you logged earlier, write a new
entry for that additional work.

This is partially automated: a git `commit`/`push` also fires a hook (`angelo memory commit`) that
flushes any pending `.memory/` files into git. The hook owns the *plumbing* (getting memory files
committed and pushed); **you** own the *content* (deciding what happened and recording it before
you commit).

## Recorded results are contracts

A recorded measurement is not a number — it is a number *plus the conditions that
make it true*: which split, over which window, against which bar. Prose loses the
conditions first. A body saying "+2.62%" survives being summarized; "on the
2019–2024 holdout, against a +2% bar" does not, and the stripped figure then reads
as unconditional.

**When an entry states a number, pass it structurally too:**

```
record(..., results_json='[{"metric":"active_return","value":2.62,"unit":"%",
                            "split":"holdout-2019-2024","window":"6y",
                            "criterion":">=2%","source":"run-114"}]')
```

`search` and `recall` surface these inline (even without `include_body`), so the
qualifiers reach the next agent in the same breath as the number.

**When you are about to state a number, name its source first.** Which entry
recorded it, and what did that entry say the split and criterion were? If you
cannot name the entry, you do not know the number — you know a residue of it, and
reasoning harder will only produce a plausible qualifier rather than the real one.
Quote splits and windows **verbatim**: "validation" and "evaluation" read as
interchangeable English but routinely name different periods.

**If `record` returns a `contradictions` block, resolve it before moving on.** The
server flags an active entry reporting a different value for the same
metric+split. Both may be right — a rerun, a fixed bug, a changed pipeline — but
the tree now asserts two answers to one question. Read the prior entry; if yours
supersedes it, re-record with `invalidates=`.

**Pre-register anything that will be judged.** Declare the bar before you see the
number, via `experiment(action='run', dry_run=true, hypothesis=...,
pass_criteria_json=..., null_interpretation=...)` in the `memory-artifacts` server.
Later runs of the same `experiment_id` inherit the criteria, so
`verify_manifest` returns a *verdict* (pass / fail / incomplete / unregistered)
rather than an interpretation. Without a pre-registered criterion a result can be
rationalized but never adjudicated — whatever the number turns out to be, some
reading of it looks like a win.

**Never invent a parameter that shapes an answer.** A date range, a split, a
threshold, a universe. If it was not specified, that is a question for the user,
not a gap to fill plausibly — an invented start date does not announce itself in
the output.

See the `answering-from-recorded-results` skill for the full sequence.

## After a compaction: re-ground before you continue

Compaction replaces your context with a summary of it, and the replacement does
not feel like a loss. You will feel oriented. Confidence is therefore useless as a
check here — it survives the loss intact, which is precisely why this needs to be
a mechanical reflex rather than a judgment call.

Angelo's `preCompact` hook snapshots what this session had touched and injects a
re-grounding brief on the next tool call. When you see it:

1. **Re-read, don't recall.** Open the entries named in the brief with
   `recall(entry_id=..., include_body=true)`. The summary's rendering of an entry
   is not the entry.
2. **Adjudicate what you are carrying.** `session(action='reground', claims=...)`
   with one claim per line — every number, filename, and conclusion you inherited
   from the summary. Claims come back as `candidates` (a pointer to read, NOT a
   verification) or `unsupported`.
3. **Treat `unsupported` as not established.** Not "probably fine but unindexed."
   A carried claim with no source is the characteristic artifact of a lossy
   summary, and it will otherwise propagate with full confidence.
4. **Distrust specificity you cannot source.** A precise figure you cannot trace to
   an entry is more suspect than a vague one, not less.

## When to Read

**Search memory before exploring the codebase.** Before you start grepping or reading files to
answer a question or scope a task, call `search` / `recall` first — prior work, decisions, or the
answer to the same question often already live in the tree. This saves time and keeps you aligned
with earlier decisions.

Read from memory when:

- Starting a new session (call `session(action='pickup')` for focused last-session context, then `recall` for the full tree)
- **Before exploring or grepping the codebase** for a question or task — check `search` / `recall` first
- Before starting work that might repeat past experiments (call `search`)
- When you need a procedure you've done before (call `skill(action='search')`)
- When navigating unfamiliar code (call `codebase`)
- **To orient on a large subtree without reading every entry**, call
  `recall(view="brief", entry_id=<phase or subproject>)` for a rollup summary
  (child titles/types, concluding checkpoint, top-central entries, most-recent
  activity, and a `completeness` score in `[0,1]`). It is deterministic (no LLM),
  cached off-file in the MCP process (a read never dirties the working tree), and
  auto-invalidates when a descendant or the node changes. Every plan node also
  carries the additive `completeness` score in the normal `recall` `open_phases`
  / node payloads — a quick "how done is this phase?" read (an intentional
  additive field; abandoned/all-discarded phases score the `0.0` floor).
  **Leaf phases** (plan nodes with no plan children) are scored directly;
  **container** nodes (subprojects, the project root) report a **rollup = the
  mean of their descendant leaf-phase scores** (so a subproject of one 0.7 phase
  reads ~0.7; a 0.7 phase + a 0.1 phase reads ~0.4). Discard-churn never inflates
  the score: only threads resolved *by a successor* (superseded) earn
  resolved-thread credit, and a retracted checkpoint is never counted (nor named
  as `concluding_checkpoint`, even under `include_discarded=true`).

## Code-Aware Editing (the `codebase` API)

`codebase(action=..., ...)` is an action dispatcher over the live code graph.
Shipped actions: `lookup` (default, name/scope search), `blast`, `callers`,
`callees`, `imports`, `context`, `ownership`, `health`. `context` returns a rolling
**tip** (the newest high-signal entry) plus an append-only provenance **chain** for a
symbol or file; `blast` returns the impacted callers/imports (each with a
`severity`) plus `tests_affected`; `ownership` git-blames a symbol's line range (or a
whole file) and returns a ranked `owners` list — author, line/commit counts, and
last-touched commit — excluding the `memory-mcp` bot. Every payload carries a `next`
hint naming the follow-on call — follow it.

**Pre-edit reflex:** before changing a symbol, call `codebase(action="context")`
to learn *why* it exists (tip + chain) and `codebase(action="blast")` to see
*what breaks*, then `record(..., files=...)` after editing so the symbol tether
advances for free. Skip this for trivial/mechanical edits (rename, typo,
formatting).

| The agent is asking… | Action |
|------|-----|
| "what breaks if I change X" | `codebase(action="blast")` |
| "why is this here / who decided this" | `codebase(action="context")` |
| "who owns / who wrote / who to ask about X" (code owners, blame) | `codebase(action="ownership")` |
| "is this risky to touch" | `codebase(action="health")` |
| "where is X defined / what lives here" | `codebase(action="lookup")` (default) |

Julia (`.jl`) and other unsupported languages degrade to file-level anchors: the
chain and tip still resolve at file grain, without `Class.method` symbols.

## Storage Model

Entries are stored as **markdown files with YAML frontmatter** in `.memory/`. The
KGLite cache (`.angelo/memory.kgl`) is disposable and rebuilt on startup. The
`.memory/` directory is the source of truth and should be committed to git; the
`.angelo/` directory holds runtime state and derived caches and is gitignored.

Entry file writes are committed in debounced batches (authored as `memory-mcp`).
Auto-push only fires when all unpushed commits are memory-mcp authored — it never
pushes user WIP commits.

Live sessions are runtime sidecars in `.memory/active-sessions/<session-id>.json`.
They include heartbeat/process metadata and the entries created, accessed, or
modified by the current MCP process. The dashboard reads these files directly so
multiple live chats can appear as separate session cards. Sidecars are ephemeral
and gitignored; finalized handoffs are written to `.memory/sessions/`.

## Research Tree Tools (MCP: memory)

| Tool | Use |
|------|-----|
| `create_project(name, description)` | Bootstrap root project (only registered while the repo has no project yet) |
| `record(project, title, body, ..., results_json?, dry_run?)` | Add an entry to the tree. Pass `results_json` whenever the entry states a number (see "Recorded results are contracts") — it keeps metric/split/window/criterion attached and lets the server flag contradictions. `dry_run=true` rehearses placement — echoes the resolved parent (and `suggested_parents` when it would default to the root) without writing, so a record lands correctly the first time |
| `discard(entry_id, reason?, dry_run?)` | Mark something as discarded (`dry_run=true` reports what would be discarded and how many active children it would orphan) |
| `move(entry_id, new_parent_id, dry_run?)` | Reparent an entry (`dry_run=true` reports the from/to reparent without writing) |
| `inbox(project?, limit?)` | Read-only "what needs attention": ranked, inert opportunities across open phases (no concluding checkpoint), drifted/stale pinned entries, and near-duplicate merge candidates. Writes nothing; each item carries its own `next` call |
| `relate(action='link', entry_a, entry_b, rationale)` | Cross-reference entries |
| `relate(action='merge', entry_a=loser, entry_b=winner, dry_run?)` | Losslessly collapse one entry into another: reparent children, union tags/files/relationships, redirect inbound references, soft-discard the loser (`merged_into:<winner>`). `dry_run=true` previews the reparented children and redirected references without writing |
| `relate(action='suggest', entry_id, project, mode='duplicate')` | Propose near-duplicate entries (high cosine + lexical-overlap confirm) as merge candidates — never auto-merges |
| `relate(action='bulk', ids, add_tags?, remove_tags?, set_type?, dry_run?)` | Batch tag/retype many entries (comma-separated `ids`) in one commit (`dry_run=true` reports which ids would change) |
| `relate(action='export')` | Export the research tree (entry nodes + `has_child`/`related` edges) as a GEXF 1.2 document for Gephi/networkx. Read-only. See [How-to → Export & import a knowledge graph](../../docs/how-to/export-import-a-knowledge-graph.md) |
| `recall(project, entry_id?, type?, view?, depth?, prose?)` | Get tree state. Returns a nested `tree` + `summary` + `open_phases` (plan nodes carry an additive `completeness` score in `[0,1]`). `view="outline"` for a plan-node scaffold (best first call on a big tree), `view="flat"` for a paginated list, `view="brief"` for a cached deterministic rollup of `entry_id`'s subtree (`prose=true` adds an opt-in LLM narrative when a generator is injected); `depth=N` collapses deep branches |
| `search(query, project?, mode?)` | Find entries by file path, keyword, or semantic similarity (`mode="hybrid"` default fuses keyword+semantic). An optional blended reranker (importance + session-recency + MMR) is off by default and tunable via `MEMORY_RERANK_*` / `MEMORY_RECENCY_HALF_LIFE_DAYS` env vars |
| `codebase(query?, scope?)` | Navigate code structure |
| `history(action='digest', granularity?, scope?, max_eras?, max_commits?, era?, include_messages?)` | Read-only: a chunked, deterministic summary of how the repo was built (eras, churn, milestones) for brownfield onboarding. See "Onboarding an existing codebase" below |
| `history(action='reconstruct', project, entries_json, dry_run?, force?)` | Bulk-persist a back-dated, `reconstructed`-tagged tree synthesized from the digest. Historical file pins via `path@sha`. Refuses a non-empty (lived) tree unless `force=true` |
| `drift(project?, action?, apply?)` | Scan pinned files for drift since pin. `action="report"` (default) classifies each drifted entry at symbol granularity (semantically stale vs spurious file-only flag); `action="sweep"` additionally tags the semantically-stale entries with a reversible `stale` tag when `apply=true` (dry run otherwise) |
| `session(action='handoff', project, summary?)` | Persist session activity for next agent (auto-called by stop hook) |
| `session(action='pickup', project, query?)` | Load previous session's focused context (plus a one-line `inbox` digest of what needs attention). No query = latest session. With query = semantic search past sessions by topic (e.g. "auto-invalidation work") |
| `session(action='reground', claims)` | Adjudicate claims you are carrying (one per line) against the record. Each returns `candidates` (a pointer to read, not a verification) or `unsupported` (treat as not established). The post-compaction reflex |

## Onboarding an existing codebase (brownfield)

Bootstrapping memory on a mature repo leaves the tree empty — `recall` has
nothing to say until months of lived recording accumulate. To close that gap,
reconstruct an approximate development history from git so the tree is useful on
day one. This is a three-stage, agent-driven flow (see the
`onboard-existing-codebase` skill):

1. **Gather** — `history(action='digest')` returns a deterministic, chunked summary of
   how the repo evolved (eras from release tags or calendar time, per-era churn,
   representative commit messages, contributors, milestones). It never dumps the
   raw log and excludes memory-mcp's own commits.
2. **Synthesize** — you (the agent) turn the digest into a proposed tree:
   subprojects for long-lived workstreams, phases for eras, checkpoints/decisions
   for milestones — each with a historical `created_at` and, where relevant, a
   `files: "path@<sha>"` pin to the commit where the change happened.
3. **Persist** — `history(action='reconstruct', project, entries_json)` bulk-writes it,
   back-dating every entry, auto-tagging it `reconstructed` / `git-history`, and
   bypassing the live-session drift machinery (a bulk import must not thrash
   sessions).

Rules of thumb:

- **Be honest about fidelity.** Reconstructed memory is a git-derived
  approximation, not lived memory. The `reconstructed` tag marks it as such;
  always show the user the proposed tree (`dry_run=true`) before writing.
- **Don't pollute a lived tree.** `history(action='reconstruct')` refuses to run when the
  tree already holds non-reconstructed entries unless `force=true`. Reserve it
  for empty/fresh trees.
- **Back-dating is intentional.** Entries carry their historical `created_at`, so
  the timeline, `open_phases` ordering, and recency ranking reflect the past.
- After reconstruction, record a **baseline checkpoint** of the current
  architecture, then capture memory live as work proceeds.

## Maintenance Tools (MCP: memory)

| Tool | Use |
|------|-----|
| `health()` | Report system health: cache freshness, counts, unpushed commits, install info |

The server auto-syncs external `.memory/` changes before each tool call, so
manual cache rebuilds are not needed. `sync` and `vacuum` exist as Python
functions (`memory.server.sync` / `memory.server.vacuum`) for rare manual
maintenance but are not exposed as MCP tools.

## Experiment Artifacts (MCP: memory-artifacts)

Large or regenerable files live in DVC (one S3 remote per repo); git keeps
manifests and lightweight pointers. Requires the `[artifacts]` extra and a
configured remote (`artifact(action='configure_remote')`). **These tools live in the
separate `memory-artifacts` MCP server** (not the core `memory` server), so the
block can be enabled or disabled on its own from Cursor's MCP settings without
affecting the core memory tools. If the tools aren't visible, check that
`memory-artifacts` is enabled.

| Tool | Use |
|------|-----|
| `experiment(action='run', experiment_id, command, artifacts_json, ...)` | Capture a provenance manifest (code SHA, data hashes, env, command); optionally execute |
| `artifact(action='backup', path)` | One-step DVC add + push + verify for a local file; the file stays where it is |
| `experiment(action='push_manifest', experiment_id)` / `experiment(action='verify_manifest', experiment_id)` | Ship or check a manifest's artifacts on the S3 remote |
| `experiment(action='rerun', experiment_id, where, dry_run)` | Rerun from a manifest in a clean sandbox at the original commit; `where="cloud"` enqueues for a runner, `where="local"` runs here |
| `experiment(action='get_job', job_id)` | Poll a queued/running rerun's state and log tail |
| `experiment(action='export_catalog', experiment_id)` | Human-readable S3 catalog beside DVC's hash-addressed cache |

Conventions:

- Experiments are series of dated runs sharing an `experiment` family id; reruns carry `rerun_of`, `executor`, and a replication check (`exact` / `divergent`)
- Rerun outputs land in `.memory/artifacts/experiments/<family>/runs/<stamp>/` and never overwrite originals or local files
- The rerun job queue lives at `<DVC remote>/jobs`; an `angelo-runner` daemon on any machine with bucket credentials executes cloud reruns
- Prefer `dry_run=true` first for any tool that touches the remote

